
			Importing data into IPW
			-----------------------



I.  Binary Raster data in GRASS, LAS or other formats:
------------------------------------------------------

Binary raster data can be input to IPW from GRASS, LAS (Land Analysis
System), or any other GIS or image processing package software which
can output data in binary raster form (byte or integer data stored in
rows or columns).


GRASS raster files
------------------

To convert a GRASS raster file to an IPW image, use the grass.to.ipw
command from GRASS:

SYNOPSIS
	GRASS> grass.to.ipw help
	GRASS> grass.to.ipw [-r] [-o] map=name [header=name] [units=name] \
	                 [divisor=value] [offset=value]	

DESCRIPTION
	grass.to.ipw converts a GRASS raster file to an IPW image file.

	A linear quantization header may be provided with -h option or stdin;
	otherwise the number of bits per pixel and floating point map
	are derived from the min and max cell values (the floating point
	map maximum may be increased to preserve the precision of the data).

	If both the -r (raw mode) flag and a header file are specified,
	the given linear quantization header will be written to the output
	image, but the data values will be copied as raw pixel values
	bypassing the floating point mapping (see examples below).

	In GRASS, the geographic region (window) specifies the north,
	south, east and west edges of the raster map.  The data values are
	assumed to be located at the cell centers so that the data value
	for the north-westernmost pixel (1,1) is actually located at
	northing NORTH-NSRES/2 and easting WEST+EWRES/2	(where NORTH
	is the northern edge of the file, WEST is the western edge of
	the file and NSRES and EWRES are the resolutions in the
	N/S and E/W directions, respectively).  In IPW, the image origin
	(beginning line and sample coordinates) are assumed to be at the
	center of pixel (0,0).  Unless the -o (override) option is set,
	the output image GEO header will be set so that:
		bline = NORTH+NSRES/2
		bsamp = WEST+EWRES/2.
		dline = -NSRES
		dsamp = EWRES
	If the -o option is specified, bline will be set to NORTH and
	bsamp will be set to west.

	The output image is written to standard output.

OPTIONS

	Flags:
	-r                  raw values (don't convert to linear quantization)
	-o		    use the GRASS NORTH and WEST values for the
			    GEO origin
			    default: origin is offset by half a pixel in
			    each direction (see discussion above)

	Parameters:
	map=name            input GRASS raster file
	header=name         input linear quantization header file
	units=name          data units for annotation
	divisor=value       divide each map data by this value
			    default: 1.0
	offset=value	    add to each map data (prior to division)
			    default: 0.0
	

EXAMPLES

	The following command,

		grass.to.ipw map=grasslayer divisor=10.0 offset=-100.0 \
		       > ipwfile

	will convert a GRASS map layer called  grasslayer to an IPW
	image file called ipwfile, subtracting 100.0 from all input
	values then dividing the result by 10.0 before storing in the
	IPW output file.

	The following pipeline of commands,

		mkbih -l 200 -s 500 -f | mklqh -m 0,0,255,1.275 -f \
		  | grass.to.ipw -r map=rawlayer > image

	creates an IPW image with 200 lines and 500 samples with linear
	floating point mapping defined by the pairs (0,0) and (255,1.275).
	The data values in GRASS raster file "rawlayer" are copied as raw
	pixel values, so that a value of 200 in input map layer grasslayer
	is copied as raw value 200 in the IPW image, and will be mapped to
	floating point value of 1.0.

	To convert several GRASS raster files into a multi-band IPW image:
		grass.to.ipw map=raster.1 > ipw.1
		    ...
		grass.to.ipw map=raster.N > ipw.N
		mux ipw.1 ... ipw.N > image



Land Analysis System (LAS) images:
----------------------------------

To convert a LAS image to an IPW image:

SYNOPSIS
	las2ipw -i lasname [-r] [-h header] [-g]

DESCRIPTION
	las2ipw converts an image file in LAS (Land Analysis System) format
	to IPW (Image Processing Workbench) format.  If an IPW header is
	provided by the -h option or stdin, the given headers will be written
	to the IPW file (except a GEO header if -g is not specified);
	otherwise the contents of the basic image (BI), geodetic (GEO) and
	linear quantization (LQ) headers are derived from the LAS DDR file.
	If both the -r flag and a header file are specified, the given
	linear quantization header will be written to the output image, but
	the data values will be copied as raw pixel values bypassing the
	floating point mapping (see examples).  The IPW output image is
	written to stdout.

OPTIONS
	-i		LAS image name
	-r		force raw pixel values to equal LAS image pixel
			values; bypass floating point conversion if used
			in conjunction with -h option
			(can't be used with REAL*4 LAS images) 
	-h		input header file (default: stdin)
	-g		flag to override LAS geographic area with GEO header
			in given header file

EXAMPLES
	Suppose lasfile.img is a LAS image with pixel data type BYTE with 
	values ranging from 0-255 and that header is an IPW file with
	an LQ header defining a linear mapping of pixel value to floating
	point with map (0,0) and (255,1.0).  The command:

		las2ipw -i lasfile -r -h header -o ipwfile

	will create an 8-bit IPW image file ipwfile with raw pixel values
	0-255 mapped to floating point 0.0 - 1.0.  The command:

		las2ipw -i lasfile -o ipwfile

	will create the IPW image file ipwfile with raw pixel values 0-255
	mapped to floating point 0.0 - 255.0.

	Suppose lasreal.img is a LAS image with data type REAL*4 and values
	ranging from 0.0 to 1000.0, and that real.head is an IPW header file
	specifying 12-bit pixels with a floating point map of
	(0,0),(4096,1000.0).  The command:

		las2ipw -i lasreal.img -h real.head -o ipwreal

	will create a 12-bit IPW image ipwreal with raw pixel values 0-4096
	mapped to floating point values 0.0-1000.0.  (Note that the pixel
	values will have the approximate values of the original LAS values
	with an error of no more than 0.244141 (1000/4096)).  The command:

		las2ipw -i lasreal.img -o ipwreal

	will create an 8-bit IPW image ipwreal with raw pixel values 0-255
	mapped to floating point values 0.0-1000.  Since no header was
	provided, an 8-bit image (default) was created.


NOTE: prior to using the las.to.ipw command, the following csh commands
need to be added to the .cshrc file or typed in at the command line:

	setenv LASROOT /usr/packages/sparc/las/las/v5_0
	setenv WORROOT /usr/packages/sparc/las/world/v2_0
	setenv TAE /usr/packages/sparc/las/tae/v1_4
	setenv response LAS50
	source $LASROOT/lasinit

	



Binary raster data in a format other than GRASS or LAS:
-------------------------------------------------------

Information needed:
	- number of lines (rows)
	- number of samples per line (columns)
	- number of bits/bytes per pixel (usually one byte, 8 bits)
	- data type: byte, integer, floating point
	- number of bands
	- if multiple bands, data format: band-sequential (BSQ), band
	  interleaved (BIL), etc.
	- size of file header, if any
	- pixel representation information - are the pixel values category
	  identifiers or can they be mapped to floating point data values?
	- map projection information (optional):
		projection name and units
		projection coordinates of upper-left pixel (center)
		pixel spacing in line and sample directions
 	- size of the image file - if the image has X samples and
	  Y lines, then the file should be exactly X*Y bytes long (if not,
	  then the image might have a header, or the records might be
	  blocked)

EXAMPLE 1
---------
To convert the "Vegitation Map of Asia" from SPANS (Spatial Analysis System)
format to IPW:

We have the following information about the SPANS file vegt.rnl:

	spatial resolution:		: 2.199 km
	number of lines:		: 3672
	number of samples:		: 5920
	number of classes:		: 36
	Projection			: Lambert Conformal Conic
		First Parallel		: 20S
		Second Parallel		: 30S
		Longitude of origin	: 85E
		Latitude of origin	: 0 N
	
	Location (decimal degrees) of the bottom left corner pixel (center):
		Longitude:	56.1086E
		Latitude:	 1.7190S

A long list (ls -l) of the file vegt.rnl shows the size to be 21738240 bytes
which is exactly 3672 * 5920 bytes.

Steps:
-----
1. Determine information for the IPW basic image (BI), linear quantization (LQ),
   and geodetic (GEO) headers:

	-BI header:
		nlines = 3672
		nsamps = 5920
		nbits = 8
		nbands = 1

		Note that since the maximum data value is 36, we could make
		this a 6-bit image (an N bit image can represent values from
		0 to 2^N - 1; 6-bits can represent values 0-63 which is
		sufficient for this case).  Some IPW programs will run faster
		with smaller bits per pixel since look-up tables will be
		shorter.

	-LQ header:
		map = 0,0,255,255

		(since this data is integer data, we want to map the values
		directly to their floating point equivalent; therefore we
		are mapping raw pixel values 0-255 to floating point values
		0.0-255.0.  If we had made the image 6-bit, the map would
		be 0,0,63,63)

	-GEO header:
		bline = 7589107
		bsamp = -3312311
		dline = 2199
		dsamp = 2199
		units = m
		csys = "Lambert Conformal Conic"

		From the file information, all we have are the projection
		parameters for the Lambert Conformal Conic map projection and
		the latitude and longitude of the center of the bottom left
		pixel.  We can obtain the Lambert coordinates in meters
		for the upper right pixel with the following steps:

			mkgeo > geo.defs
			mklamcc -p -10,-30 -o 0 -c 85 > lamcc.defs
			echo "56.1086 -1.7190" | \
				transcoord -i geo.defs -o lamcc.defs
			  (output is -3312311 -485621 which is the Lambert
			   easting and northing for the bottom left pixel)

		Then bline = bottom northing + nlines * dline
			     -485621 + 3672 * 2199 = 7589107
		and bsamp = left easting = -3312311

2.  Create the IPW image with the headers defined above - first create the
headers in a file using the -f (no image copy) option, then append the
image data to the file:

	mkbih -l 3672 -s 5920 -f | mklqh -m 0,0,255,255 -f | \
		mkgeoh -o 7589107,-3312311 -d -2199,2199 -u meters
			-c "Lambert Conformal Conic" -f > vegt.ipw
	cat vegt.rnl >> vegt.ipw


EXAMPLE 2
---------
To convert an AVHRR satellite image tape of the Northwest United States:

We have the following information about the file w1.172:

	Data Type:	Byte
	Number Lines:	948
	Number Samples:	1070
	Data Format:	BSQ
	Number Bands:	4

	Pixel Size:	1101
	Projection:	Albers conical equal area
	Center of pixel (1,1)   X = -2384766.0 meters
				Y = 3192900.0 meters

	Bands: 1 2	Units:  0.25% Albedo
	Bands: 3 4	Units:  0.50 deg K (0 = 190 deg K)

The file size is 4057520 bytes.

Steps:

1.  The image should be nbands * nlines * nsamps, or 4 * 948 * 1070 = 4057440,
so our file is 80 bytes too long.  By examination of the file with btoa, it
is determined that there is an 80-byte header on the file that must be
stripped before processing:

	dd if=w1.172 bs=80 skip=1 > avhrr.dat

2. Determine information for the IPW basic image (BI), linear quantization (LQ),
   and geodetic (GEO) headers:

	-BI header (all 4 bands):
		nlines = 948
		nsamps = 1070
		nbits = 8

	-LQ header (bands 1, 2 (IPW bands 0, 1))
		map = 0,0,255,0.6375
		units = "%Albedo/100"

		The first two bands contain albedo in units of 0.25%.
		This means raw pixel value 0 represents 0%, 1 represents
		.25%, ..., and the maximum raw pixel value 255 = 255 * .25%
		= 63.75% = 0.6375

	-LQ header (bands 3, 4 (IPW bands 2, 3))
		map = 0,0,190.0,317.5
		units = "deg K"

		The second two bands contain temperature in units of 0.5
		deg K above 190 deg K.  This means raw pixel value 0
		represents 190 deg K, 1 represents 190.5 deg K, ...,
		and the maximum raw pixel value 255 represents 255 * .5 + 190
		= 317.5 deg K.

	-GEO header (all 4 bands):
		bline = 3192900
		bsamp = -2384766
		dline = 1101
		dsamp = 1101
		units = m
		csys = "Albers conical equal area"


3.  To create the IPW images with the headers defined above:
    Since the image has four bands in BSQ format, the first 948 * 1070
    = 1014360 bytes are band 1 data, the second 1014360 bytes are band 2
    data, etc.  We can use the dd command to split off the data for each
    band, appending the data to the header files:

	mkbih -l 948 -s 1070 -a "AVHRR band 1: Albedo" -f \
		| mklqh -m 0,0,255,0.6375 -u "%Albedo/100" -f \
		| mkgeoh -o 3192900,-2384766 -d -1101,1101 -u m \
			-c "Albers conical equal area" -f > band1.ipw

	mkbih -l 948 -s 1070 -a "AVHRR band 2: Albedo" -f \
		| mklqh -m 0,0,255,0.6375 -u "%Albedo/100" -f \
		| mkgeoh -o 3192900,-2384766 -d -1101,1101 -u m \
			-c "Albers conical equal area" -f > band2.ipw

	mkbih -l 948 -s 1070 -a "AVHRR band 3: Temperature" -f \
		| mklqh -m 0,190,255,317.5 -u "deg K" -f \
		| mkgeoh -o 3192900,-2384766 -d -1101,1101 -u m \
			-c "Albers conical equal area" -f > band3.ipw

	mkbih -l 948 -s 1070 -a "AVHRR band 4: Temperature" -f \
		| mklqh -m 0,190,255,317.5 -u "deg K" -f \
		| mkgeoh -o 3192900,-2384766 -d -1101,1101 -u m \
			-c "Albers conical equal area" -f > band4.ipw

	dd if=avhrr.dat bs=1070 count=948 >> band1.ipw
	dd if=avhrr.dat bs=1070 skip=948 count=948 >> band2.ipw
	dd if=avhrr.dat bs=1070 skip=1896 count=948 >> band3.ipw
	dd if=avhrr.dat bs=1070 skip=2844 count=948 >> band4.ipw


4.  If desired, the four files can be merged into one 4-band image file
    using the mux command:

	mux band1.ipw band2.ipw band3.ipw band4.ipw > avhrr.ipw




II.  Raster data in ASCII format:
---------------------------------

The import program can be used to import ASCII data for a rectangular region
stored in row order:

NAME
	import -- import ASCII data file to an IPW image

SYNOPSIS
	import -l nlines -s nsamps [-b nbands] [-n nbits[,...]] [-i infile]

DESCRIPTION
	Import reads in an ASCII file of data points and constructs an IPW
	image.  The data values are assumed to be in band-interleaved-by-pixel,
	row by column order.  The data values are read free-format; any
	number of bands/samples per input file line.

OPTIONS
	l	# lines in output image
	s	# samples in output image
	b	# bands in output image (default is 1)
	n	# bits for each band (default is 8)
	i	input file (defaults to stdin)

OPERANDS

EXAMPLES

	Suppose file input.dat has the following format:

	0	0.5	2.2	1.3	6.5	12.2
	21.5	3.5	-1.5	0.1	13.4	-1.9

	The following command:

		import -l 6 -s 2 -i input.dat > image

	creates an image with 6 lines, 2 samples per line where the
	first lines contains samples 0 and 0.5, line 2 contains samples
	2.2 and 1.3, etc.




III.  Generating interpolated surfaces from point data
------------------------------------------------------

The surface program can be used to generate an IPW image containing the
results of surface interpolation from ASCII point data (input file with
lines containing easting/longitude northing/latitude data_value):

NAME
	surface -- interpolate image from irregularly spaced point data

SYNOPSIS
	surface [-n #points] [-h header] [-m mask] [-p infile]

DESCRIPTION
	surface reads in an ASCII file of data points and constructs an IPW
	image by spatial interpolation using an inverse-distance squared
	weighting algorithm.  The number of nearest neighbors used in the
	interpolation may be specified (default: 12).  The given header
	file will be used to determine the number of lines, samples, bits
	per pixel (in the BIH header) and geographic referencing information
	(GEO header).  If the header file contains a linear quantization
	header, it will be used for floating point mapping in the output
	image; otherwise the mapping will be derived from the data.  The
	ASCII input file contains easting (x-coordinate), northing 
	(y-coordinate) data value triplets where the northing and easting
	are in the same projection units as the given GEO header.

OPTIONS
	n	# neighbors to use in interpolation (default: 12)
	h	IPW header file (default: stdin)
		Note: at least one of (-h,-p) must be specified
	m	mask image
	p	points input file (default: stdin)
		Note: at least one of (-h,-p) must be specified


Note: if the ASCII data files contains locations in one projection (for example
latitude/longitude), and the desired projection for the output image is a
different projection (for example Albers), use the transcoord program to
convert the coordinates of the ASCII file:

NAME
	transcoord -- translate map coordinates to different map projection

SYNOPSIS
	transcoord -i inproj -o outproj [infile]

DESCRIPTION
	transcoord translates a file of geographic projection coordinates
	to another map projection using the USGS General Cartographic
	Transformation Package (GCTP) software.  The input file is an 
	ASCII file with one easting, northing pair per line, followed by
	any other data.  The output file, written to stdout, is an ASCII
	file with the input map coordinates replaced by the translated
	coordinates, and the remainder of the data line copied.  The input
	and output projection parameters are defined in files inproj (-i
	parameter) and outproj (-o parameter) respectively (see below
	for file format).

OPTIONS
	-i	input projection definition file

	-o	output projection definition file

OPERAND
	infile	input file (default:stdin)

EXAMPLE

FILES
	The projection definition files are ASCII files with the following
	format:
		projection ID
		units code
		zone code
		datum code
		projection parameters (15 values)

	The files may be created with programs mkgeo, mkalbers, etc.  See
	the USGS documentation for the General Cartographic Transformation
	Package for details on the parameters.
